## 3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD

    DOMINATING PREDICATES of the edited lines, walked up to the enclosing function only:
        relative_addr >= <WIN> && relative_addr < <NEXT>      (window selection)
        len != 8                                             (width)
        writeflag == MEM_WRITE                               (direction; the edit is its `else')

    FIELDS THE GUARDED BODY READS:
        relative_addr        -> the index expression
        len                  -> the read loop's bound (TLB arms only; LINEAR is fixed-arity)
        d->re_tlb_a/b/c[],
        d->re_linear_a/b[]   -> the values returned
        d->warned            -> TEX only, the latch

    WHERE THEY DIFFER, and whether written at the same sites:

      len          guard TESTS it (`!= 8'); body USES it as a loop bound.  A mutant can shorten
                   the loop without touching the guard.  COVERED: a short loop leaves odata's
                   high lanes unfilled, so the value differs and the 6 TLB `ld' rows redden.

      d->warned    read by the TEX body's own latch test; WRITTEN AT 8 SITES, six of them inside
                   DEVICE_ACCESS -- so ordinary guest register traffic sets bits in this field.
                   *** THIS IS THE DISAGREEING-STATE CASE AND IT OWES A ROW. ***  Shipped as
                   `tex xbit': fire all six width latches with narrow writes FIRST, then read
                   TEX.  If the decline were given an existing bit rather than SGIRE_W_TEX_RD its
                   message would already be suppressed and the row reds.

    *** THE ENUMERATION ABOVE IS INCOMPLETE, AND THREE SEATS FOUND THE SAME MISSING CELL
    ON PASS 2: `odata'. ***

      The guarded ACCEPT body READS it -- `odata = (odata << 16) | cell' on the TLB arms,
      and the LINEAR arms assign it -- and the function's common tail CONSUMES it.  So it
      is a body-read lvalue inside the closure as I defined it, walked no further than
      DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re), and it should have been listed beside `writeflag', `len',
      `relative_addr', the arrays and `d->warned'.

      **THE COVERAGE IS NOT AFFECTED -- ONLY THE STATEMENT WAS.**  `M2_datafill' IS the
      odata-cell mutant: it swaps the destination from `odata' to `data[]', which the tail
      then overwrites.  It scored 42/44 and KILLED.  In grok's words, *"M2 is the existence
      proof -- you nearly shipped the swap to data[] -- and it is not generated by the
      listed names."*

      So this is a WRONG RECORD (L4), corrected here, and not a floor breach: the cell has
      a mutant and the mutant dies.  **What it cost is that the enumeration could not have
      GENERATED its own strongest mutant** -- M2 came from having made the mistake, not
      from working the closure.  A future round applying this block mechanically would have
      missed it.

      *Found independently by codex, glm and grok.  The convergence is the reason it is
      recorded as a defect in the method rather than as a stylistic quibble.*

    JOINT CELLS (the body-tested-partition rule):
      accept arms  the guarded body contains NO `if' at all -- it is a loop and an assignment.
                   The partition is trivial, so there are no joint cells to enumerate.  N/A by
                   construction, not unchecked.
      TEX          the body tests exactly one lvalue, `d->warned & SGIRE_W_TEX_RD', giving two
                   cells {latch clear, latch set}.  BOTH ROWED: `tex ld lo' enters with it clear,
                   `tex rd lat1' and `tex xbit' with it set.


## 4. CHANNEL -- per row, the mutant it kills and the observable that differs under that mutant

A row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous BY CONSTRUCTION, and that
is knowable now, before any table runs.

    ROW                 KILLS (mutant)                        OBSERVABLE THAT DIFFERS
    ------------------  ------------------------------------  ------------------------------------
    live64              a mis-specified 64-bit want format;    the printed v0 on a path that
                        a broken sd/ld through RAM             cannot die -- this is the row that
                                                              makes the other 14 interpretable
    <arm> ld lo   x5    verbatim reinstatement of exit(1);     v0: the stored value vs death or a
                        read-side index re-map; lane swap;     wrong/zero value.  PLUS the store's
                        a data[] fill instead of odata;        own debug naming the index (clause
                        a whole-window translation             (f) anchor) -- absent under a
                                                              translation, since the store's index
                                                              text moves with it
    <arm> ld hi   x5    all of the above, AND a FAR-BOUND      same channel at the window's LAST
                        shrink of the window                   slot.  A base-only row cannot see a
                                                              far-bound move at all
    tex ld lo/hi        TEX converted to ACCEPT; the decline    v0 == 0 AND the count of the TEX
                        literal deleted or renamed             decline literal.  *** THE STORE IS
                                                              WHAT MAKES THIS NON-VACUOUS: without
                                                              it an ACCEPT of the zeroed array
                                                              returns 0 too and the row cannot
                                                              redden ***
    tex rd lat1         the latch removed (announce per        literal count over TWO reads at two
                        access rather than per instance)       DIFFERENT offsets -- so a latch
                                                              keyed on the address also reds
    tex xbit            SGIRE_W_TEX_RD replaced by an          literal count after all six width
                        already-used bit (SGIRE_W_TEX_LEN      latches are set: 1 vs 0
                        or any *_LEN)

    *** NO ROW BELOW READS EXIT STATUS. ***  `#499` measured `how == "exit0"' vacuous at a
    refusal-effect site: an exit(0) reinstatement prints its message and then dies, scoring green
    with the process dead.  Survival here is implied by the VALUE -- a dead session prints none.


## 4b. THE LATCH-INDEPENDENCE CELL -- ADDED ON PASS 2 (L2, floor breach, repaired in-round)

The CHANNEL table above lists `tex xbit' as killing *"SGIRE_W_TEX_RD replaced by an already-used
bit (SGIRE_W_TEX_LEN or any *_LEN)"*.  **The parenthesis is the defect: `tex xbit' fires the six
WIDTH latches only, so it sweeps bits 0-5 and cannot see a collision with any other bit.**

codex named the escape and predicted its score against the 44-row detector:

    SGIRE_W_TEX_RD = 1 << 11   ->   1 << 8   (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)      predicted 44/44

with the reason drawn from the detector's own session schedule: **the four TEX-read scenarios and
the unmapped-read scenario never share an emulator instance**, so bit 8 is clear in every session
that reads TEX.  `M10' established detection of a WIDTH-bit collision, not of every existing-bit
collision -- which is the same over-generalisation the parenthesis made.

    ROW                 KILLS                              OBSERVABLE THAT DIFFERS
    ------------------  ---------------------------------  --------------------------------
    tex/unmap           SGIRE_W_TEX_RD colliding with any  both decline literals present
    unmap/tex           OTHER used bit, in either order    exactly once, in ONE instance

**Both orderings are rowed deliberately.**  One would leave the mirror open -- the `#499' shape,
where a repair closes the cell the mutant used and leaves its symmetric twin for the next pass.

## 5. CARRY-FORWARD

`#490` lost two rows this block had prescribed when a later panel rewrote the addition list.
**Any later rewrite of this round's row set is DIFFED against the five rows named in section 4
(`live64`, the lo/hi pairs, the four TEX rows), not merely extended with whatever the pass-2 panel
finds.**  The block prescribes rows; a list that does not contain them has dropped an obligation.
